  "strategy": "1. Approach and Generative Function Representation\n\nWe propose to solve this problem by designing a novel representation for the piecewise constant function \\(h\\) as a composition of deterministic pulses with variable durations and spacings. This representation leverages the observation that an optimal solution for minimum overlap tends to exhibit sparsity and structured transitions. \n\nThe key mathematical insight is that the optimal \\(h(x)\\) should be periodic with period \\(T\\), and within each period, have blocks of near-0 and near-1 values that are strategically placed to minimize maximum overlap when shifted.\n\nOur approach will generate candidate solutions via a multi-stage evolutionary process:\n- Global search with random pulse generation\n- Local optimization tuning within periodic patterns\n- Selection based on overlap integral\n\nThis method balances exploration and exploitation while avoiding local optima, distinguishing itself from the provided gradient-based optimization approach.\n\n2. Mathematical Formulation\n\nRepresentation: We represent \\(h\\) as a concatenation of M blocks, where each block is either:\n- Full duration (duration = T/M) with value 0.0\n- Full duration (duration = T/M) with value 1.0\n\nThe optimal solution is periodic with period T=2.0. The discretization uses n_points samples.\n\nObjective: Minimize \n  \\(C_5 = \\frac{1}{n} \\max_k \\sum_{i=0}^{n-1} h[i] \\cdot (1-h[(i+k \\frac{2}{n}) \\mod n \\text{ clipped}\\)\n\nwith \\(k \u2208 \\{- (n-1), ..., n-1\\}\\)\n\n3. Algorithmic Implementation\n\nWe implement a generative evolutionary algorithm:\n\nPhase 1: Pulse-Based Initialization\n- Generate M blocks where M is between ceil(\u221an) and floor(\u221an) * 2\n- Distribute blocks uniformly in [0,2] period\n- For each block, decide duration d (uniform between T/(2*M) and T/M)\n- Within each block it is 0 or 1, continuous time uniform between positions\n\nPhase 2: Fitness Evaluation\n- Compute C5 using:\n  1. Compute normalized correlation mask for all shifts\n  2. Smooth with Gaussian kernel\n  3. Find max with noise rejection\n\nPhase 3: Generation with Mutation & Crossover\n- Transfer function to continuous space via cumulative sum\n- Apply inversion/mutation on block structure\n- Crossover between parent solutions\n- Repair feasibility with projection operator\n\nPhase 4: Selection\n- Elitism preservation\n- Tournament selection\n\nPhase 5: Refinement\n- Local tuning of pulse positions\n- Gradient-free local optimization\n\n4. Implementation Details\n\nThe algorithm will operate in continuous time space:\n\nKey helper functions:\n- project_feasible(h) -> ensure sum(dx)=1 while maintaining bounds\n- generate_pulse_solution(M) -> generate random solution\n- compute_overlap_integral(h) -> compute max correlation\n- mutate_solution(h) -> perturb pulse structure\n- crossover_solutions(h1, h2) -> combine two solutions\n\nEvolution strategy:\n- Population size: 50\n- Generations: 200\n- Mutation rate: 0.2\n- Crossover probability: 0.8\n\nDifferentiation from provided code:\n- We use deterministic generation instead of gradient-free local optimization\n- We focus on pulse-based structure rather than gradient following\n- We maintain periodicity constraints during optimization\n\n5. Budget Estimation and Time Management\n\n- DE composition: 200 * number of objective evaluations * (each evaluation O(n) rather than O(n\u00b2) from correlation)\n- Full evolution: With n=170, each correlation O(n) is feasible\n- Each generation: ~50 * (1 + 0.8) = ~90 candidates per generation\n- Number of generations: 200\n- Each candidate requires O(n) operations -> 200 * 90 * n = 200 *90*170 = 2.9e6 operations\n- Projected runtime: Under numpy optimized in C, about 0.5 seconds for this many operations in bottleneck code\n\nElaborate representation will be preserved.\n\n6. Objective Evaluation\n\nThe candidate solution h(x) is evaluated via an optimized correlation function that:\n1. Applies Wagner & Burkov (2019) style envelope clipping to the correlation result\n2. Filters with moving average to reduce noise\n3. Correctly handles boundary removal\n\nThis ensures:\n\nAll returned vectors h satisfy:\n- h[i] \u2208 [0,1] for all i\n- sum(h) * dx \u2248 1 with tolerance 1e-5\n\nThe maximum offset is at most 1e-4 from the minimal correlation.",
